<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<handout xml:id="worksheet-4">
  <title>Worksheet 4: Continuity, Discontinuity, and the Intermediate Value Theorem - Sections 2.5a and 2.5b</title>

  <page>
    <paragraphs xml:id="ws4-prob1">
      <title>Problem 1</title>
      <p>
        Show that <m>\displaystyle f(x)=\frac{x^2-5}{2x^3-7}</m> is continuous at <m>x=1</m> by:
      </p>
      <ol marker="(a)">
        <li workspace="1.1in">
          <p>Using the limit definition of continuity.</p>
        </li>
        <li workspace="0.8in">
          <p>Using the fact of continuous functions (see Day 6 - Page 4).</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="ws4-prob2">
      <title>Problem 2</title>
      <p>
        Find and classify all discontinuities of the following functions. Be sure to justify your answers!
      </p>
      <p workspace="1.5in">
        <strong>(c)</strong> <m>\displaystyle h(x)=\frac{|x^2-4|}{x^2-x-2}</m>. <em>Hint:</em> Note that <m>|a \cdot b| = |a| \cdot |b|</m> and
        <me>|x| = \begin{cases}
        x \amp \text{if } x \geq 0 \\[6pt]
        -x \amp \text{if } x \lt 0
        \end{cases}</me>
      </p>
      <p workspace="1.8in">
        <strong>(d)</strong>
        <me>k(x) = \begin{cases}
        5 - e^{x-1} \amp \text{if } x \lt 1 \\[6pt]
        4 \amp \text{if } x = 1 \\[6pt]
        \sqrt{x} - \ln(x) + 3 \amp \text{if } 1 \lt x \lt 5 \\[6pt]
        \dfrac{x}{(x-9)^2} \amp \text{if } x \geq 5
        \end{cases}</me>
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="ws4-prob4">
      <title>Problem 4</title>
      <p>
        Sketch or provide an example of a function that has each of the following sets of characteristics, or explain why such a function cannot exist.
      </p>
      <ol marker="(a)">
        <li workspace="1in">
          <p><m>f(x)</m> has a jump discontinuity at <m>x=1</m> but is continuous everywhere else. <em>Hint: Try sketching such a function and draw a conclusion based on your observation.</em></p>
        </li>
        <li workspace="1in">
          <p><m>f(x)</m> has an infinite discontinuity at <m>x=2</m> and a removable discontinuity at <m>x=5</m>. <em>Hint: Try sketching such a function and draw a conclusion based on your observation.</em></p>
        </li>
        <li workspace="1in">
          <p><m>f(x)</m> is a rational function of the form <m>\displaystyle \frac{ax^2+bx+c}{dx^2+ex+f}</m>, where <m>a</m> and <m>d</m> are nonzero constants, that is continuous everywhere. <em>Hint: When is a rational function not defined? Then use the fact of continuous functions.</em></p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </paragraphs>
  </page>

  <page>
    <paragraphs xml:id="ws4-extra-heading">
      <title>Additional Practice Problems (to be completed outside of studio)</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="ws4-extra-prob2">
      <title>Problem 2 (cont.)</title>
      <p>
        Find and classify all discontinuities of the following functions. Be sure to justify your answers!
      </p>
      <ol marker="(a)">
        <li workspace="1in">
          <p><m>f(x) = 2x^2 - 4e^{2x} + 7\cos(x) - 3</m></p>
        </li>
        <li workspace="1.3in">
          <p><m>\displaystyle g(x) = \frac{x^2-1}{x^2-7x+6}</m></p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="ws4-extra-prob3">
      <title>Problem 3</title>
      <p>
        Consider the function <m>f(x) = x^2 - 2x - 4</m>. Use the Intermediate Value Theorem (IVT) to answer the following questions. <em>Caution: It's possible for a function to do something even when the Intermediate Value Theorem doesn't say it has to!</em>
      </p>
      <ol marker="(a)">
        <li workspace="1.2in">
          <p>Use the IVT to show that <m>f(x)</m> is equal to <m>-2</m> for some <m>x</m> on the interval <m>[0,3]</m>. Find such value(s) of <m>x</m>.</p>
        </li>
        <li workspace="1.2in">
          <p>Find <m>f(0)</m> and <m>f(2)</m>. Can the IVT tell you about the behavior of <m>f(x)</m> on the interval <m>[0,2]</m>?</p>
        </li>
        <li workspace="1.2in">
          <p>Now look at <m>f(4)</m>. What can the IVT guarantee about the root of <m>f(x)</m> on the interval <m>[0,4]</m>?</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="ws4-extra-prob4">
      <title>Problem 4 (cont.)</title>
      <p>
        Sketch or provide an example of a function that has each of the following sets of characteristics, or explain why such a function cannot exist.
      </p>
      <p workspace="1.1in">
        <strong>(d)</strong> <m>f(x)</m> goes through the points <m>(0,1)</m> and <m>(5,2)</m>. <em>Hint: Try sketching such a function and draw a conclusion based on your observation.</em>
      </p>
      <p workspace="1.1in">
        <strong>(e)</strong> <m>f(x)</m> is continuous on <m>[0,5]</m>, goes through the points <m>(0,-1)</m> and <m>(5,2)</m>, and is never equal to <m>0</m>. <em>Hint: Try sketching such a function and draw a conclusion based on your observation.</em>
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </page>
</handout>